    "ogAccent": "7A2E83",
    "heroCredit": "Rap Grid",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://www.rapgrid.com/leagues/queen-of-the-ring",
    "body": "Everybody points at the big URL marquee when battle rap comes up. Real talk — the sport's deepest, most slept-on lane is Queen of the Ring.\n\nQOTR is Black-owned and women-led, and it's been the home for the best women in the game for years. Not a side stage. The main one for an entire division the culture barely covers.\n\n## Why it's the move this summer\n\nBattle rap's summer season is heating up across every league, and the timeline does the announcing now — cards get built in public on X, matchup by matchup. QOTR's roster reads like a who's-who the mainstream never learned: pens that would humble a lot of the names people throw around as the best.\n\nThis is the mission in one beat. Show love to hip-hop in the places the charts ignore. Battle rap is one of those places. The women's division inside it is even further off the radar. So we point right at it.\n\n## What to watch\n\nKeep your eyes on the QOTR account as the summer cards firm up. The league's history is the receipt — when QOTR puts two top-tier writers across from each other, it's the kind of bar-for-bar exchange that built the whole sport before the cameras showed up.\n\nThe pen still rules. The league still belongs to us. And the part of it nobody's filming is usually where the best rhyming is happening.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Rap Grid — Queen of the Ring](https://www.rapgrid.com/leagues/queen-of-the-ring)\n- [VerseTracker — QOTR battles](https://versetracker.com/battles/qotr-queen-of-the-ring)\n- [Queen of the Ring on X](https://x.com/THEQOTR)",
