    "body": "If you've ever loved a Final Fantasy logo, a character splash, that watercolor-feeling art that made the games look like myth — that's Yoshitaka Amano. Now he's getting his own anime, and the first trailer hits Anime Expo on July 3.\n\nZAN adapts DEVA ZAN, Amano's long-running dark-fantasy project. This isn't a one-off illustration. It's a universe he's been building across more than 300 original works, finally moving into animation.\n\n## Why the name carries weight\n\nAmano is one of the most influential artists in the entire medium. His fingerprints are on Final Fantasy, on Vampire Hunter D, on a whole visual language that shaped how fantasy anime looks. When a creator at that level finally adapts his own world, that's not a niche premiere — that's a big-name event, even before anybody's seen a frame move.\n\nThe question is whether the animation can carry that singular style without flattening it. Amano's work lives in texture and stillness. Putting it in motion is the whole challenge, and the whole reason to watch the trailer.\n\n## What to watch\n\nJuly 3 at AX for the first trailer. This is one for the anime heads who care about craft, not just hype — the people who'll recognize the hand before they read the credit.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Outlook Respawn — Amano's ZAN trailer at AX 2026](https://respawn.outlookindia.com/pop-culture/pop-culture-news/yoshitaka-amanos-zan-anime-gets-trailer-at-anime-expo-2026)\n- [ComicBook — Top Anime Expo 2026 reveals](https://comicbook.com/anime/list/top-5-anime-expo-2026-reveals-were-most-excited-about/)\n- [Anime Expo — official](https://www.anime-expo.org/)",
