    "title": "The $300 Sampler Just Got USB Audio",
    "headline": "Teenage Engineering's Pocket Sampler Levels Up",
    "subhead": "Firmware 2.5 turns the EP-133 K.O. II into a real desktop interface — USB audio in and out, sample reverse, an arpeggiator, 40-second mono samples — all free for anybody who already owns one.",
    "description": "Teenage Engineering's EP-133 K.O. II firmware 2.5 adds USB audio in/out, reverse, an arpeggiator and 40-second samples — free. The standing GEAR beat.",
    "keywords": [
      "Teenage Engineering EP-133",
      "EP-133 K.O. II firmware 2.5",
      "USB audio sampler",
      "beatmaking gear 2026",
      "pocket sampler",
      "Sneakz and Beatz",
      "PHRHX GEAR beat"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "THE POCKET\nSAMPLER\nLEVELS UP",
    "ogAccent": "F5A623",
    "heroCredit": "Synth Anatomy",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://synthanatomy.com/2026/06/teenage-engineering-ep-133-k-o-ii-2-5-new-firmware-adds-usb-audio-reverse-and-more.html",
    "body": "The standing GEAR beat ties straight to the beat business, and this one's for everybody who makes beats on a budget.\n\n## USB audio changes the workflow\nThe June 24 firmware 2.5 update adds USB audio in and out to the EP-133 K.O. II. Plug it into any class-compliant host — your laptop, your phone — and pull audio straight in or record its output directly into a DAW. No interface, no cables hunt. For a sub-$300 box, that's the feature that moves it from toy to tool.\n\n## Reverse, arp, and longer chops\nThe update also brings sample reverse, an arpeggiator that runs in one-shot or legato mode, and mono samples up to 40 seconds — double the room you used to get. There's a new auto-chop too that slices a sample into even divisions automatically, so flipping a loop into playable hits takes seconds instead of manual trimming.\n\n## Free is the flex\nNone of this costs a dollar. Teenage Engineering pushed 2.5 to existing owners for free. In a market where companies nickel-and-dime you for feature unlocks, a free update that genuinely expands what the hardware does is rare — and it's exactly the kind of move that keeps a piece of gear in the culture.\n\n## Why it matters for the beat biz\nThe whole point of a cheap, deep sampler is access. A kid with $300 and a phone can now record, chop, reverse, arpeggiate, and bounce straight to a session. That's the same pipeline a placement runs through. Cheaper tools, fewer excuses.\n\n## Sources\n- [Synth Anatomy — EP-133 K.O. II firmware 2.5](https://synthanatomy.com/2026/06/teenage-engineering-ep-133-k-o-ii-2-5-new-firmware-adds-usb-audio-reverse-and-more.html)\n- [Gearnews — EP-133 K.O. II update: USB audio, reverse & more](https://www.gearnews.com/teenage-engineering-ep-133-k-o-ii-sampler/)\n",
    "publishedAt": "2026-06-28",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-07-28T17:45:03.582Z"
