    "pillar": "hiphop",
    "body": "Gear beat ties straight to the beat business, so here's one worth your money. Akai put out its most accessible standalone sampler yet, and it's built on the boxes that made the sound.\n\n## What it is\nThe MPC Sample is a portable, battery-powered unit priced at $399. It draws its DNA from the MPC60 and MPC3000 — the machines that shaped decades of hip-hop drums — and strips the workflow down so anybody can sample, chop and arrange with no computer in the room. It's small enough to live in a backpack and runs up to five hours on a rechargeable lithium-ion battery.\n\n## Why the hype is real\nMusicTech called it the most-hyped gear of the year, and the demand backs that up — it sold through and came back in stock in the EU. The pull is simple: the MPC name at a price a bedroom producer can actually reach, with the portability the big flagships don't give you.\n\n## Where it sits in the lineup\nThis is the opposite end of the shelf from the MPC XL flagship. The XL is the do-everything centerpiece; the Sample is the grab-it-and-go. If you make beats on the move, this is the one, and it's a clean tie-in to cop a beat from the vault while your own setup's still coming together.\n\n## What to watch\nStock has been the story — watch restock waves in the US, and watch whether Akai expands the sound library and firmware the way it did on past standalones.\n\n## Sources\n- Synth Anatomy — Akai MPC Sample hardware sampler\n- MusicTech — how the MPC Sample became the most-hyped gear of 2026\n- Sound On Sound — Akai Pro unveil the MPC Sample",
