    "heroCreditUrl": "https://www.gematsu.com/",
    "body": "Phantom Blade Zero slipped its date — but only a little. S-Game moved the 'kung-fu punk' action RPG from September 9 to October 29, and opened pre-orders for this summer, all confirmed at a State of Play. A short push, not a disappearing act.\n\nThis one's been in trailer purgatory for a minute, so a firm new date with pre-orders live is actually good news dressed as a delay. Six weeks is nothing when the alternative is another year of 'coming soon.'\n\n## What you're actually playing\n\nYou're Soul, an assassin on a John Wick-style revenge run. The pitch is fast, dense, stylish combat — a deep weapon system, brutal bosses, and a movement game that leans on the 'kung-fu punk' aesthetic the studio's been selling since the reveal. It's built for the crowd that likes their action tight and their difficulty real.\n\n## Why a short delay reads fine\n\nDelays get treated like disasters. This isn't one. Moving from early September to late October is the kind of slide studios make to dodge a crowded window or buff the final polish. With pre-orders opening the same summer, the confidence signal is there — they're taking money, which means they believe in the date.\n\n## Where it sits in the RPG year\n\nMost of the genre's heavy hitters are still years out. A dated, nearly-here action RPG on PS5 and PC is worth paying attention to precisely because it's real and close. For the single-player crowd this show lives in, October 29 is a date to circle.\n\nThe swords looked mean the whole time. Now they've got a day attached. That's the win.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Phantom Blade Zero launches September 9, 2026 — original date (Gematsu)](https://www.gematsu.com/2025/12/phantom-blade-zero-launches-september-9-2026)\n- [Phantom Blade Zero moved to October 29 + summer pre-orders (official X)](https://x.com/pbzero_official/status/2061938441185821154)",
