    "body": "Host lane, and it's the nearest one on the board: Forsaken Realms: Vahrin's Call drops July 27. The pitch is a straight-up mash of two RPG heavyweights — Fable's free-form build customization and whimsical British humor, plus the gritty, story-first feel of The Witcher 3.\n\nThe devs have been rolling out a YouTube miniseries breaking down the core systems, which is exactly how our gaming picks get chosen.\n\n## Why the YouTube rollout matters\n\nWe pick gaming by what's actually pulling views, then filter to the single-player big-world lane. A dev dropping a feature-by-feature miniseries and getting real traction is a signal — people are engaged before launch. That's more telling than a trailer that goes quiet.\n\n## The AA sweet spot\n\nThis isn't a $250M blockbuster and it isn't pretending to be. It's aimed at players who want a full open-world RPG that leans a little scrappier — more personality, less polish-for-polish's-sake. That AA lane has produced some of the most beloved RPGs of the last few years precisely because it takes swings the big studios won't.\n\n## Nearest, so it leads the wait\n\nWith Fable still a Fall-2026 target and most big RPGs further out, a dated July 27 release you can actually play is worth more than a dozen 'coming soon' reveals. Blend two of the best RPG formulas ever, ship it this month, and you've got the host's attention.\n\nSometimes the game you can play beats the game you're waiting on.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [The Witcher meets Fable in new RPG coming July 27 (ScreenRant)](https://screenrant.com/witcher-meets-fable-new-rpg-july-2026/)\n- [Upcoming RPGs for 2026 and beyond (GamesRadar)](https://www.gamesradar.com/upcoming-rpg-games/)",
