    "slug": "queen-of-the-ring-black-owned-league-2026",
    "pillar": "hiphop",
    "title": "Queen of the Ring: The Black-Owned Lane",
    "headline": "The First Room.\nStill Standing.",
    "subhead": "Battle rap's biggest women's stage is Black-owned and built from scratch — this beat keeps the light on Queen of the Ring.",
    "description": "Queen of the Ring is the first women's battle rap league — Black-owned, founded in 2010 by Debo, Vague, and Babs Bunny. Why it still matters today in 2026.",
    "keywords": [
      "Queen of the Ring",
      "QOTR battle rap",
      "women's battle rap league",
      "Black-owned battle league",
      "Babs Bunny Debo Vague",
      "battle rap 2026",
      "Black battle rappers",
      "Sneakz and Beatz"
    ],
    "ogHeadline": "The first room.\nStill standing.",
    "ogAccent": "C0392B",
    "heroCredit": "Rap Grid",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://www.rapgrid.com/leagues/queen-of-the-ring",
    "body": "Off the timeline, our battle beat keeps the light on the rooms the mainstream skips. This one goes to Queen of the Ring — QOTR — the platform that built the women's side of this sport from nothing.\n\nIt's Black-owned. It's still running. And it's still the standard.\n\n## Where it started\n\nQueen of the Ring launched in 2010 in New York City, founded by Debo, Vague, and Babs Bunny. It's widely credited as the first women's battle rap league of its kind — a lane that didn't exist as a real stage until they made one. Sixteen years later it's still the room where the top women in the sport earn their names.\n\n## Why the ownership matters\n\nWe talk a lot about showing love to hip-hop past the chart-pop version of it. A Black-owned league that carved out an entire category is exactly that story. Nobody handed QOTR a platform; they built the platform, then built the pipeline of talent that runs through it. That's culture ownership, not culture rental.\n\n## The bigger point\n\nBattle rap already lives outside the mainstream. The women's side has had to fight for coverage inside a scene that's itself underground. Keeping QOTR in the conversation — alongside the URLs and the RBEs — is how you tell the full story of who's actually moving this art form.\n\nWhen the next card locks, we'll bring you the tale of the tape. For today, respect where it's due: the first room, still standing.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Queen of the Ring — league profile (Rap Grid)](https://www.rapgrid.com/leagues/queen-of-the-ring)\n- [Queen of the Ring (rap battle league) — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_of_the_Ring_(rap_battle_league))",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-16",
    "goLiveAt": "2026-07-28T17:46:41.582Z"
