    "body": "Anime only makes this show when it's a big-name title. This one clears the bar on the byline alone.\n\n## The name behind it\nDaemons of the Shadow Realm is the anime adaptation of Yomi no Tsugai, the manga by Hiromu Arakawa — the same mind that gave the world Fullmetal Alchemist. For a lot of fans, FMA and FMA: Brotherhood sit permanently in the top-five conversation. So a new Arakawa world getting animated isn't a niche premiere; it's an event.\n\n## Where and when to watch\nNetflix put it up worldwide on July 4, 2026, with new episodes weekly on Saturdays. By now it's several episodes deep — enough to tell whether it's got the world-building teeth the pedigree promises, and early looks say the DNA is there: twin-bound spirits, a village sitting on a secret, and stakes that widen fast.\n\n## Why it lands\nArakawa's whole thing is grounded fantasy — magic with rules, characters with real weight, a plot that pays off setups instead of hand-waving them. That's what made FMA age like almost nothing else in the medium. If Daemons carries even part of that discipline, it's the sleeper of the summer slate.\n\n## The read\nYou don't need a trailer breakdown to know why this matters. The person who made Fullmetal Alchemist made something new, and it's a click away on Netflix. Start there.\n\n## Sources\n- What's on Netflix: Daemons of the Shadow Realm release date — https://www.whats-on-netflix.com/news/anime/daemons-of-the-shadow-realm-netflix-release-date/\n- CBR: Fullmetal Alchemist's successor launches on Netflix — https://www.cbr.com/netflix-daemons-of-the-shadow-realm-july-2026/\n- Hiromu Arakawa — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiromu_Arakawa",
