    "body": "Every episode carries a gear beat, because it ties straight to the beat business. This one's for the producers who chase feel over features.\n\n## What it is\nThe Akai MPC Sample is a compact, battery-powered hardware sampler built around the character of the MPC60 and MPC3000 — the machines that defined boom bap. It just came back around in stock, and the demand tells the story: MusicTech called it the most-hyped gear of 2026.\n\n## Roger Linn in the room\nThe pull isn't nostalgia for a logo. Roger Linn — the man behind the original Linn drum designs and the early MPC feel — is tied to the project, and that's why heads care. Swing, timing, the way a sample sits in the pocket: that's what people are chasing here.\n\n## Why it fits the vault\nUp to five hours on a battery, chop-and-sequence with no laptop, sampling front and center. That's the exact workflow behind a boom-bap catalog — grab a loop, chop it, let the pads do the talking. It's a producer's tool, not a menu-diver's.\n\n## The bigger point\nStandalone boxes keep winning in 2026 because they get you out of the screen and into the feel. The MPC Sample leans all the way into that — sampling first, menus last.\n\n## Sources\n- Synth Anatomy — Akai MPC Sample, inspired by the MPC60 and MPC3000\n- MusicTech — How Akai's MPC Sample became the most-hyped gear of 2026",
