    "ogAccent": "1F7A5A",
    "heroCredit": "Synth Anatomy / Superbooth",
    "heroCreditUrl": "https://synthanatomy.com/",
    "body": "Most drum machines make you pick a side — analog warmth or sample accuracy. Polyend's answer at Superbooth 2026 is to stop picking.\n\n## What it is\nPolyend Drums is a standalone eight-track drum machine, and each track can pull from analog voices, digital synthesis, or your own samples. Layer them however you want, then run Polyend's sequencing on top — the kind of pattern tools the brand built its name on. One box that covers boom-bap grit and modern sound design without a laptop in the chain.\n\n## Why a beatmaker cares\nThe MPC world is about chopping. Analog boxes are about feel. Polyend is betting you want both in the same groove — a kick with real analog punch under a chopped-up sample and a synth clap you dialed in yourself. That flexibility is the pitch.\n\n## The move\nIt's a reveal, so lock the ship date and street price before you plan a purchase. But the concept is the story: a serious drum machine that refuses to be just one thing. For a producer building a catalog, range like that earns its rack space.\n\n## Sources\n- Sound On Sound — grooveboxes & drum machines: https://www.soundonsound.com/sequencers-trackers-grooveboxes\n- Synth Anatomy — drum machine news: https://synthanatomy.com/category/music-tech-news/news-this-way-best-hardware-music-tech-news-by-synth-anatomy/drum-machines",
